\subsection{Risk register (residual risks)}
Likelihood (L) and impact (I) are rated low, medium or high for the lab context and for a hypothetical production use; the owner is the work area of Appendix~I that follows the risk.
\tcap{Risk register}
\begin{center}\scriptsize
\begin{longtable}{@{}L{0.6cm}L{3.6cm}L{0.7cm}L{0.7cm}L{4.4cm}L{4.4cm}L{0.9cm}@{}}
\toprule\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Risk} & \textbf{L} & \textbf{I} & \textbf{Existing mitigation} & \textbf{Planned action} & \textbf{Area}\\\midrule\endhead
R1 & Web process compromised: it holds the actor key and the three database passwords, so it can act as any user again & L & H & Read-only code, unprivileged worker, no published DB port, second factor in front of the privileged roles; incident 2 shows a stolen \emph{password} is contained & Split into a front end and an identity and audit service; separate the keys; WebAuthn & C, E\\
R2 & O01: the login helper returns hashes to a compromised runtime account & L & M & Argon2id; helper limited to one row per e-mail; second factor for privileged roles & Narrower credential interface; rate-limit the helper & C\\
R3 & TOTP is not phishing-resistant; researchers and reviewers have no second factor & M & M & Throttle, pair lock, replay protection, recovery codes, alerts & WebAuthn; extend the second factor to every role & B\\
R4 & Distributed guessing from many addresses against one researcher or reviewer account & M & M & 5 per address per 15 min; alert at 20 failures per hour & Second factor for all roles; monitoring & B\\
R5 & People behind one address share the throttle & M & L & Thresholds are configuration values; 10-minute window & Per-user risk scoring; trusted-proxy rule & B\\
R6 & Keyring, actor key, passphrase and private backup key start on one lab host; no QR enrolment & M & H & Custody scripts; key backup as a separate file; setup key and otpauth address shown & Two custodians; offline media; rotation calendar; a QR code if a library is approved & E\\
R7 & Docker or host administrator is trusted & L & H & Read-only code, 0600 secrets, no published DB port & Accepted for the lab; separate duties in production & D\\
R8 & CI runs the project's own checks only; the image job reports pending upgrades but blocks nothing; patch cadence is manual & M & M & CI on every push and pull request, dependabot, rebuild on 17 November (0 pending), clean-room check & Make the patch report a gate; vulnerability scanner when available; scheduled rebuilds & D, E\\
R9 & E-mail goes to files in the lab; a real SMTP server and per-user rate limits are configuration, not demonstrated & M & L & Fixed templates, no details, header checks, TLS-only credentials, retries, one message per event & Operate and monitor a real relay; per-user limits & D\\
R10 & No WAL archiving; backups on the same host & L & H & Verified restore; encrypted, checksummed backups & Off-site copy; point-in-time recovery & D\\
R11 & Connection cap and Argon2id memory under load (about 15 concurrent requests) & M & M & Throttle answered before hashing; statement and lock timeouts & Connection pooling; bound Apache workers; load test & D\\
R12 & Audit trail is tamper-resistant, not tamper-evident; no retention; after the fix the server log is the only record of an out-of-band attacker & L & M & Triggers and grants; separate writer; server log with statement text & Hash chain or external shipping; \code{pgaudit}; partitioning & D\\
\bottomrule\end{longtable}\end{center}

\subsection{Limits of this assessment}
The tests are bounded lab exercises on fictional data, not a certification. Timing was measured locally, without network jitter. A distributed guessing attack against one researcher or reviewer account is detected, not prevented (privileged roles also need a second factor, which is not phishing-resistant). TLS certificates are self-signed; e-mail goes to files in the lab. A Docker administrator is trusted. Patch status: the 8 (web) and 5 (db) pending package upgrades were applied on 17 November by rebuilding with pulled base images (now 0 and 0), but nothing keeps them current automatically. The system is single-node and was not load-tested: with each request able to use two runtime connections and a limit of 30, roughly 15 simultaneous requests is the ceiling. All key material, including the actor key, starts on one lab host. A compromise of the web process, which holds the actor key, is not contained by the database.

%% ====================================================================== 19. RECOMMENDATIONS
\section{Recommendations}\label{sec:recs}
\tcap{Recommendations}
\begin{center}\small
\begin{longtable}{@{}L{0.70cm}L{11.65cm}L{2.51cm}@{}}
\toprule\textbf{\#} & \textbf{Recommendation} & \textbf{Status}\\\midrule\endhead
1 & Split the web process into a front end and an identity and audit service, so that no single process holds the actor key, the database passwords and the application code (closes the residual of O02/O03) & Open\\
2 & WebAuthn (phishing-resistant) for officers and auditors, a second factor for researchers and reviewers, and a QR code for enrolment if a library is approved & Open\\
3 & Ship the PostgreSQL log and the audit trail to a separate host; add \code{pgaudit}; alert on \code{permission denied} for \code{grants_app} that matches no HTTP request & Open\\
4 & Make the audit trail tamper-\emph{evident} (hash chain or external shipping) and define retention and partitioning & Open\\
5 & Turn the CI image patch report into a gate and add a vulnerability scanner when one is available; scheduled rebuilds (\code{docker compose build --pull}) & Partly done: CI configured; report informational\\
6 & Connection pooling or a higher limit, and a bound on Apache workers, so Argon2id memory and the 30-connection cap are not exhausted under load & Open\\
7 & Off-site backup copy and WAL archiving for point-in-time recovery; production certificates from a trusted CA; move the backup private key and the actor key to separate custody & Open\\
8 & A narrower view of user columns for the runtime role; a narrower credential helper (O01); a trusted-proxy rule before any reverse proxy is added & Open\\
9 & Operate a real SMTP relay and per-user mail limits; keep the templates free of proposal details & Open\\
10 & F01 award function; F02--F04 throttle and equal work; F05 headers; O02 and O03 (actor token, audit door); second factor; database-enforced deadlines; CSV export; outbox; CI; key rotation, key backup, custody tools & \fixed\\
\bottomrule\end{longtable}\end{center}

%% ====================================================================== 20. CONCLUSION
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:concl}
The project demonstrates the whole security process of the assignment on one realistic system. The design puts integrity rules in the database, authorization in two independent layers and secrets under a documented key lifecycle. The attack phase found real weaknesses -- a partial-award path, an account-lockout denial of service, a timing gap, missing throttling, a missing header, and two trust boundaries (a self-asserted identity and a writable audit trail) -- and, as the assignment requires, each was explained by its root cause and impact, fixed, and verified by replaying the same probe; the stolen-credential incident was run before and after the fix. The SQL-injection laboratory showed both the damage an injection can do and how privileges limit it. The two incidents show what the evidence can and cannot prove, and name the controls that held and the ones that did not.

What we would claim, and no more: the delivered application resisted every attack we tried within a bounded scope, the database refuses what the roles may not do even when the application is bypassed, and a lost database can be restored with its privileges and encrypted content. What remains is documented and prioritized: the web process that holds the actor key, phishing-resistant authentication for every role, tamper-evident audit and log shipping, patch cadence and load behaviour.

