\subsubsection*{C.5 Detailed hardening assessment (Section~\ref{sec:harden})}
\begin{center}\scriptsize
\begin{longtable}{@{}L{2.21cm}L{3.78cm}L{4.14cm}L{4.51cm}@{}}
\toprule\textbf{Item} & \textbf{Before (measured defaults)} & \textbf{After (measured)} & \textbf{Why this decision}\\\midrule\endhead
Default accounts & \code{postgres} superuser, \code{trust} for local and loopback in \code{pg_hba.conf} & \code{postgres}: \code{peer} on the local socket only, no network line; first-match rules end with \code{host all all all reject} & The superuser must not be a network identity; the container OS user is the administrator\\
Unnecessary users & Only the superuser exists & Exactly six login roles (\code{grants_app}, \code{grants_audit_write}, \code{grants_auditor}, \code{grants_notifier}, \code{grants_backup}, \code{postgres}) with connection limits 30, 10, 5, 3, 2 & One account per purpose makes every connection attributable in the server log\\
Excessive privileges & \code{PUBLIC} may connect to a new database: true & PUBLIC connect and schema use: false; runtime not owner, no BYPASSRLS; F01 closed & Least privilege (Section~\ref{sec:lp})\\
Weak application accounts & --- & Random 48-hex passwords (runtime, auditor, audit writer, notifier, backup), SCRAM-SHA-256, TLS-only, set with statement logging off, passed by environment variable & Passwords must not appear in command lines or logs\\
Network exposure & \code{listen_addresses = *}; \code{host all all all scram-sha-256}; SSL off & No published DB port; \code{hostssl ... samenet}; web bound to 127.0.0.1:8080/8443; TLS 1.3 in use; \code{pg_hba} rejects superuser and non-TLS from the web network & A compromised container still cannot reach other accounts or databases\\
Logging & \code{log_connections} unset, \code{log_disconnections} off, \code{log_lock_waits} off, \code{log_statement} none & Connections, disconnections, lock waits on; \code{log_statement = ddl}; errors log the failing statement & Needed for incident reconstruction (Section~\ref{sec:incident}); DML logging was not enabled because it would copy sensitive parameters into the log\\
Configuration files & Image defaults & \code{pg_hba.conf}, \code{hardening.conf}, \code{php.ini}, \code{vhost.conf} versioned in the repository and mounted read-only; \code{expose\_php} Off, \code{display\_errors} Off, \code{allow\_url\_fopen} Off & Configuration is reviewable and reproducible\\
Unnecessary features & Apache: 28 modules, \code{autoindex} and \code{status} on, \code{ServerTokens OS}, signature on & \code{autoindex}, \code{status}, \code{serve-cgi-bin}, default site disabled; \code{ServerTokens Prod}; \code{TraceEnable Off}; 29 modules loaded (adds \code{ssl}, \code{headers}) & Least functionality; no banner or directory listing\\
File permissions & Worker \code{www-data}; no session policy & Worker \code{grantsweb}; sessions 0700; code and config mounted read-only; \code{secrets/} 0700 and files 0600; \code{server.key} 0600; 43 ignored paths, 0 tracked secret files & A web-process compromise cannot rewrite code or read other secrets\\
Patch and update status & --- & PostgreSQL 18.7, PHP 8.5.12, Apache 2.4.69 after the rebuild of 17 November. Before it, \code{apt-get -s upgrade} listed 8 pending package upgrades in the web container and 5 in the db container; after \code{docker compose build --pull}, a pulled \code{postgres:18} and recreated containers it lists 0 and 0 (data volume kept, a fresh encrypted backup taken first, the 52 tests and the recovery drill rerun) & Patched on 17 November; images are only as current as their last rebuild, so rebuild on a schedule (Section~\ref{sec:recs})\\
Backup protection & No backup & Public-key encrypted, SHA-256, 0600; field key backed up separately; private key passphrase-protected and exportable & Section~\ref{sec:data}, \ref{sec:backup}\\
\bottomrule\end{longtable}\end{center}


